243
00:13:29,310 --> 00:13:32,390
corticosteroids, and
decongestants. Also,

244
00:13:32,390 --> 00:13:35,010
illicit drug use,
specifically cocaine,

245
00:13:35,050 --> 00:13:37,650
can be a common cause
of hypertension.

246
00:13:39,950 --> 00:13:42,990
And it's one that you
might see, like, on board

247
00:13:42,990 --> 00:13:47,130
exams. Sometimes I like
to use the cocaine use.

248
00:13:47,570 --> 00:13:50,830
And then there's
pheochromocytomas,

249
00:13:51,230 --> 00:13:53,470
primary aldosteronism.

250
00:13:53,610 --> 00:13:57,210
So primary aldosteronism
is going to be

251
00:13:57,210 --> 00:13:59,630
the presence of primary
mineral corticoid

252
00:13:59,630 --> 00:14:03,130
excess, specifically
aldosterone, and usually

253
00:14:03,130 --> 00:14:05,290
presents with a triad
of hypertension,

254
00:14:05,710 --> 00:14:08,870
unexplained hypokalemia,
and metabolic alkalosis.

255
00:14:09,290 --> 00:14:12,450
Then there's obstructive
sleep apnea. So

256
00:14:12,450 --> 00:14:14,830
this one is commonly
seen in patients that

257
00:14:14,830 --> 00:14:17,350
have obesity, and
they're going to have

258
00:14:17,350 --> 00:14:19,470
disordered breathing
during their sleep.

259
00:14:19,550 --> 00:14:22,870
And lastly, there's
stenosis of the renal artery

260
00:14:22,870 --> 00:14:27,030
and fibromuscular dysplasia.
So these renal vascular

261
00:14:27,030 --> 00:14:30,310
diseases are typically
presenting with a sound of

262
00:14:30,310 --> 00:14:32,910
an abdominal bruit when
you do that abdominal exam.

263
00:14:32,910 --> 00:14:36,250
And then specifically for
fibromuscular dysplasia,

264
00:14:36,410 --> 00:14:40,330
this usually occurs
in younger adult

265
00:14:40,330 --> 00:14:43,810
females, and you'll
see a string of beads

266
00:14:43,810 --> 00:14:47,810
appearance of the
renal artery on CT.

267
00:14:50,430 --> 00:14:53,890
Then we have white coat
hypertension, which is

268
00:14:53,890 --> 00:14:56,670
when the blood pressure
readings are going to

269
00:14:56,670 --> 00:14:59,810
be high in office, but
then when they measure

270
00:14:59,810 --> 00:15:02,680
it at home, it's
normal. and then masked

271
00:15:02,680 --> 00:15:04,790
hypertension, which is
going to be the opposite.

272
00:15:04,790 --> 00:15:08,050
So in office, the
blood pressure readings

273
00:15:08,050 --> 00:15:10,270
are normal, but when
they take their blood

274
00:15:10,270 --> 00:15:12,750
pressure out of
office, it's elevated.

275
00:15:14,640 --> 00:15:17,010
So in the case of white coat

276
00:15:17,010 --> 00:15:20,190
hypertension and
masked hypertension,

277
00:15:20,650 --> 00:15:23,370
we would use the ambulatory

278
00:15:23,370 --> 00:15:25,070
blood pressure measurement.

279
00:15:25,150 --> 00:15:28,750
So like we mentioned
before, it's this kind of

280
00:15:28,750 --> 00:15:32,650
cuff that's worn over the
course of 24 to 48 hours.

281
00:15:32,950 --> 00:15:35,830
So the patient keeps it
on and then that cuff

282
00:15:35,830 --> 00:15:37,550
keeps measuring the blood
pressure about every

283
00:15:37,550 --> 00:15:42,370
30 minutes. So this is
used when we see a patient

284
00:15:42,370 --> 00:15:45,890
that has some kind of
discrepancy between their

285
00:15:45,890 --> 00:15:48,410
home and office blood
pressure measurements.

286
00:15:50,370 --> 00:15:54,230
Next we have resistant
hypertension. So this is defined

287
00:15:54,230 --> 00:15:57,050
as when the blood pressure
remains greater than or

288
00:15:57,050 --> 00:16:01,850
equal to 140 over 90,
despite use of three or more

289
00:16:01,850 --> 00:16:06,470
antihypertensive medications
of different classes, including

290
00:16:06,470 --> 00:16:09,890
a diuretic at maximum
tolerated dose, or when the

291
00:16:09,890 --> 00:16:12,970
blood pressure is achieved
through 4-plus medications.

292
00:16:13,090 --> 00:16:15,810
So that would mean that
it would be considered

293
00:16:15,810 --> 00:16:17,870
a controlled resistant
hypertension.

294
00:16:18,040 --> 00:16:21,630
So some of the causes of
this, like we talked about

295
00:16:21,630 --> 00:16:23,770
before, could be secondary
hypertension. hypertension,

296
00:16:23,950 --> 00:16:27,570
so this could be potentially
something that was

297
00:16:27,570 --> 00:16:30,750
missed, and you'd have to
kind of go back and do some

298
00:16:30,750 --> 00:16:32,830
more lab work, redo
your physical exam more

299
00:16:32,830 --> 00:16:35,570
thoroughly to see, you know,
what could be causing it.

300
00:16:35,790 --> 00:16:37,810
Another cause could
be noncompliance with

301
00:16:37,810 --> 00:16:40,810
medications. If the
patient's not taking them as

302
00:16:40,810 --> 00:16:43,210
prescribed, we can get
resistant hypertension.

303
00:16:43,390 --> 00:16:47,730
And also diet.
If the patient is

304
00:16:47,930 --> 00:16:50,470
noncompliant with lifestyle
changes, and they're

305
00:16:50,470 --> 00:16:52,890
supposed to decrease
their sodium, however,

306
00:16:53,170 --> 00:16:56,370
they continue to eat a
lot of fast food diet,

307
00:16:56,370 --> 00:16:59,690
then this could be
another potential cause.

308
00:17:01,310 --> 00:17:05,910
And then there's hypertensive
crisis. So there's two

309
00:17:05,910 --> 00:17:09,310
different categories within
hypertensive crisis. The

310
00:17:09,310 --> 00:17:13,330
first is hypertensive urgency.
So this is severe hypertension,

311
00:17:13,330 --> 00:17:15,710
meaning that the systolic
is greater than or

312
00:17:15,710 --> 00:17:19,230
equal to 180, and the diastolic
is greater than or equal

313
00:17:19,230 --> 00:17:24,410
to 120, with no signs of
acute end organ damage. And

314
00:17:24,410 --> 00:17:27,510
then there's hypertensive
emergency. So this is severe

315
00:17:27,510 --> 00:17:32,090
hypertension. Again, same
greater than or equal to 180

316
00:17:32,450 --> 00:17:36,810
over greater than or equal
to 120 with evidence of

317
00:17:36,810 --> 00:17:40,310
acute end organ damage. So
some of the things that this

318
00:17:40,310 --> 00:17:43,310
can include are going to be
signs of encephalopathy or

319
00:17:43,310 --> 00:17:47,130
stroke, signs of retinal
hemorrhages or papilledema,

320
00:17:47,350 --> 00:17:50,790
signs of myocardial
infarction, heart failure,

321
00:17:51,010 --> 00:17:52,350
aortic dissection,

322
00:17:52,610 --> 00:17:55,750
kidney injury. So
these are some of the

323
00:17:55,750 --> 00:17:57,450
things you want to keep
in mind if your patient

324
00:17:57,450 --> 00:18:00,090
comes in with very
elevated blood pressure

325
00:18:00,090 --> 00:18:02,370
into the emergency
room or into clinic.

326
00:18:04,310 --> 00:18:07,430
Some of the causes of
hypertensive crisis

327
00:18:07,430 --> 00:18:10,410
can include a
secondary hypertension,

328
00:18:10,530 --> 00:18:14,090
any kind of medication
non-compliance, sudden

329
00:18:14,090 --> 00:18:17,530
discontinuation of short-acting
antihypertensive medications,

330
00:18:17,530 --> 00:18:20,950
So, for example, beta
blockers or clonidine,

331
00:18:21,330 --> 00:18:26,370
MAO inhibitors and tyramine
-rich foods, and recreational

332
00:18:26,370 --> 00:18:29,650
drug use. So, the common
one would be cocaine use.

333
00:18:31,630 --> 00:18:35,870
So, primary hypertension
risk factors can be divided

334
00:18:35,870 --> 00:18:39,670
into modifiable or non
-modifiable risk factors.

335
00:18:39,930 --> 00:18:43,330
So modifiable are going
to be the things that you

336
00:18:43,330 --> 00:18:45,910
can work on with your
patients and counsel them on

337
00:18:45,910 --> 00:18:50,230
to try and improve. And
then the non-modifiable risk

338
00:18:50,230 --> 00:18:52,330
factors are going to
be the things that your

339
00:18:52,330 --> 00:18:55,290
patient can't really change
about their life. So the

340
00:18:55,290 --> 00:18:57,790
modifiable are going to
include things like obesity,

341
00:18:57,790 --> 00:19:01,470
a high-sodium or unhealthy
diet, any excess alcohol

342
00:19:01,470 --> 00:19:05,030
intake, physical
inactivity, and smoking. And

343
00:19:05,030 --> 00:19:08,910
then the non-modifiable is
going to include increased

344
00:19:08,910 --> 00:19:11,670
age, any sort of family
history of hypertension,

345
00:19:12,030 --> 00:19:14,910
race, and low
socioeconomic status.

346
00:19:15,010 --> 00:19:18,610
So with race, something
to keep in mind is that

347
00:19:18,630 --> 00:19:21,450
specifically for your
black patients is going to

348
00:19:21,450 --> 00:19:26,150
be that they have more
common, more severe,

349
00:19:26,150 --> 00:19:29,350
occurring earlier in life
hypertension, and it can

350
00:19:29,350 --> 00:19:31,910
be associated with greater
target organ damage.

351
00:19:31,910 --> 00:19:34,390
So you want to be very
mindful of this when

352
00:19:34,390 --> 00:19:37,190
you're evaluating your
patients of different race.

353
00:19:37,630 --> 00:19:41,690
So all of these risk
factors go in hand with the

354
00:19:41,690 --> 00:19:45,630
cardiovascular disease risk
factors. And so decreasing

355
00:19:45,630 --> 00:19:48,690
those modifiable risk
factors is going to

356
00:19:48,690 --> 00:19:52,190
decrease the risk of
hypertension and in turn also

357
00:19:52,190 --> 00:19:55,570
decrease the risk of
cardiovascular disease in your

358
00:19:55,570 --> 00:19:59,510
patients. This is why it's
very important work in

359
00:19:59,510 --> 00:20:02,590
trying to help your
patient out with decreasing

360
00:20:02,590 --> 00:20:07,010
those modifiable risk factors
as a future physician.

361
00:20:08,870 --> 00:20:11,550
So now let's think
back to our case

362
00:20:11,550 --> 00:20:12,930
from the beginning
of the lecture.

363
00:20:13,130 --> 00:20:14,870
Think about what
risk factors that

364
00:20:14,870 --> 00:20:17,730
patient had and
which of those are

365
00:20:17,730 --> 00:20:19,830
modifiable and which
are not modifiable.

366
00:20:21,510 --> 00:20:25,630
So some of the modifiable
risk factors that our

367
00:20:25,630 --> 00:20:29,670
patient had included NSAID
use, a sedentary lifestyle,

368
00:20:29,830 --> 00:20:32,030
fast food intake,
which is probably

369
00:20:32,030 --> 00:20:33,550
going to be high in sodium,

370
00:20:33,670 --> 00:20:38,250
our patient was a smoker
and had alcohol intake,

371
00:20:38,250 --> 00:20:41,630
and a high BMI. So these
are the things you can

372
00:20:41,630 --> 00:20:44,390
counsel this patient on.
Some of the non-modifiable

373
00:20:44,390 --> 00:20:47,470
risk factors are going to
include a family history

374
00:20:47,470 --> 00:20:50,330
of hypertension hypertension
as well as race.

375
00:20:51,830 --> 00:20:54,830
So now we have a few
practice questions for you

376
00:20:54,830 --> 00:20:57,730
guys. So for the first
one, a 49-year-old woman

377
00:20:57,730 --> 00:21:00,210
presents for an annual
physical. She has no

378
00:21:00,210 --> 00:21:02,750
history of hypertension,
diabetes, or any cardiovascular

379
00:21:02,750 --> 00:21:06,390
disease, exercises
regularly, has a healthy

380
00:21:06,390 --> 00:21:09,910
diet, doesn't smoke,
doesn't drink alcohol. Her

381
00:21:09,910 --> 00:21:13,230
vitals include blood
pressure of 128 over 84,

382
00:21:13,490 --> 00:21:17,630
heart rate of 72,
respiratory rate 12, and

383
00:21:17,630 --> 00:21:21,290
temperature of 98.4 degrees
Fahrenheit. Replete blood

384
00:21:21,290 --> 00:21:24,310
pressure readings on two
subsequent visits are

385
00:21:24,310 --> 00:21:26,550
similar. Which of the
following is the correct

386
00:21:26,550 --> 00:21:29,010
classification of this
patient's blood pressure?

387
00:21:29,010 --> 00:21:32,990
Is it normal, elevated,
hypertension stage 1,

388
00:21:32,990 --> 00:21:36,650
hypertension stage 2, or
white coat hypertension?

389
00:21:38,980 --> 00:21:42,150
So the correct answer is
going to be C, hypertension

390
00:21:42,150 --> 00:21:45,330
stage 1. If we take a look
at this patient's blood

391
00:21:45,330 --> 00:21:48,770
pressure, their systolic
of 128 falls under the

392
00:21:48,770 --> 00:21:51,790
category of elevated,
whereas the diastolic of 84

393
00:21:51,790 --> 00:21:56,730
falls under the category
of stage 1 hypertension. So

394
00:21:56,730 --> 00:21:59,510
this is just to remind
you that if there's a

395
00:21:59,510 --> 00:22:02,470
discrepancy between
categories in the systolic and

396
00:22:02,470 --> 00:22:04,470
diastolic, we're going to
go with the higher category.

397
00:22:07,590 --> 00:22:10,750
So next we have a 60-year
-old white male with past

398
00:22:10,750 --> 00:22:13,710
medical history of
hypertension, hyperlipidemia,

399
00:22:13,850 --> 00:22:16,890
type 2 diabetes, and heavy
tobacco use presenting to

400
00:22:16,890 --> 00:22:19,150
the clinic for follow-up
of the high blood pressure.

401
00:22:19,150 --> 00:22:22,170
Despite escalating his
antihypertensive regimen, he

402
00:22:22,170 --> 00:22:24,610
has persistently elevated
blood pressure readings.

403
00:22:25,030 --> 00:22:27,290
The only symptom he
has is a headache. He

404
00:22:27,290 --> 00:22:29,790
denies any chest pain.
He's currently taking

405
00:22:29,790 --> 00:22:33,610
four antihypertensive
medications. BMI is 25.

406
00:22:33,850 --> 00:22:36,350
Vitals are unremarkable,
except his blood

407
00:22:36,350 --> 00:22:40,410
pressure is 177 over 120
in the right arm and 179

408
00:22:40,410 --> 00:22:43,550
over 123 in the left
arm. Physical exam is

409
00:22:43,550 --> 00:22:46,330
consistent with an upper
abdominal burry. The

410
00:22:46,330 --> 00:22:48,330
finding is best
explained by which of the

411
00:22:48,330 --> 00:22:51,550
following, aortic correcation,
aortic dissection,

412
00:22:51,670 --> 00:22:54,510
abdominal aortic
aneurysm, renal artery

413
00:22:54,510 --> 00:22:57,330
stenosis, or
obstructive sleep apnea.

414
00:22:59,110 --> 00:23:05,070
So our patient has a
couple of risk factors, has

415
00:23:05,070 --> 00:23:07,730
persistently elevated
blood pressure readings,

416
00:23:08,620 --> 00:23:12,440
so remember if you're taking
three or more antihypertensive

417
00:23:12,440 --> 00:23:15,810
medications you are
considered to have a

418
00:23:15,810 --> 00:23:19,010
resistant hypertension and
one of the causes that we

419
00:23:19,010 --> 00:23:23,790
discussed was secondary
hypertension so we want to start

420
00:23:23,790 --> 00:23:28,550
looking at those underlying
causes. Now on his physical

421
00:23:28,550 --> 00:23:32,510
exam he had an upper
abdominal buoy so that kind

422
00:23:32,510 --> 00:23:35,830
of clues us in more towards
a renal artery stenosis.

423
00:23:38,050 --> 00:23:41,030
Next, we have a 25-year
-old female who presents

424
00:23:41,030 --> 00:23:43,050
to the emergency department
with an episode of

425
00:23:43,050 --> 00:23:46,190
epistaxis associated with
extremely elevated blood

426
00:23:46,190 --> 00:23:49,550
pressure. She has no past
medical history. Her blood

427
00:23:49,550 --> 00:23:52,690
pressure is noted to be
170 over 100 in the ED.

428
00:23:52,730 --> 00:23:56,750
BMI is 24. On physical
exam, there are no heart

429
00:23:56,750 --> 00:23:59,570
murmurs, no abdominal
bruise, no thyromegaly.

430
00:23:59,570 --> 00:24:02,610
Lab workup, including
TSH, serum electrolytes,

431
00:24:02,610 --> 00:24:04,910
blood glucose, and
creatinine is normal.

432
00:24:05,050 --> 00:24:07,050
which of the following
additional tests would you

433
00:24:07,050 --> 00:24:10,130
order next? An ultrasound
of the renal arteries,

434
00:24:10,490 --> 00:24:13,010
polysomnography
for sleep apnea,

435
00:24:13,010 --> 00:24:14,710
a urine drug screen, a full

436
00:24:14,710 --> 00:24:16,930
thyroid panel,
or a lipid panel.

437
00:24:18,530 --> 00:24:23,810
So this patient is presenting
with epistaxis, has

438
00:24:23,810 --> 00:24:26,670
high blood pressure when
has had no prior history,

439
00:24:27,090 --> 00:24:30,770
is a young patient, no
physical exam findings

440
00:24:30,770 --> 00:24:34,570
that are remarkable, no
remarkable lab workup,

441
00:24:34,870 --> 00:24:39,690
findings. So this is kind
of cluing us in more towards

442
00:24:39,900 --> 00:24:43,150
looking into secondary
causes of hypertension.

443
00:24:43,150 --> 00:24:46,270
Remember, we discussed like
one of the things you'll look

444
00:24:46,270 --> 00:24:52,710
into is young patients with
no history. And so this is

445
00:24:52,710 --> 00:24:56,550
kind of cluing us more
towards potential cocaine use.

446
00:24:56,550 --> 00:24:59,950
So the answer choice would
be C, urine drug screen.

447
00:25:00,070 --> 00:25:04,690
For the other answer
choices, for A, ultrasound

448
00:25:04,690 --> 00:25:06,350
of the renal arteries
would be helpful in

449
00:25:06,350 --> 00:25:08,890
diagnosing a renal artery
stenosis, but that's

450
00:25:08,890 --> 00:25:10,950
something we more so
see in older patients.

451
00:25:11,170 --> 00:25:13,250
And on physical exam,
we'd probably hear an

452
00:25:13,250 --> 00:25:16,490
abdominal burry present.
Then for answer choice B,

453
00:25:16,490 --> 00:25:19,250
polysomnography for sleep
apnea. That's incorrect

454
00:25:19,250 --> 00:25:22,050
because usually we
see a patient that has

455
00:25:22,050 --> 00:25:25,470
a much higher BMI. It's
seen in obese patients.

456
00:25:25,810 --> 00:25:30,450
Answer choice D, a full
thyroid panel. So our patient

457
00:25:30,450 --> 00:25:35,250
already had normal TSH, had
no thyromegaly on physical

458
00:25:35,250 --> 00:25:38,210
exam, so it's more unlikely
and probably unnecessary

459
00:25:38,210 --> 00:25:41,250
to go through with ordering
a full thyroid panel.

460
00:25:41,250 --> 00:25:46,130
And then answer choice E,
a lipid panel, that would

461
00:25:46,810 --> 00:25:51,710
more so be indicated
for a patient that we're

462
00:25:51,710 --> 00:25:54,890
doing an initial workup
for, for hypertension.

463
00:25:55,390 --> 00:25:59,330
So again, it's probably
more unlikely. The more

464
00:25:59,330 --> 00:26:04,070
correct answer would be to
check the urine drug screen.

465
00:26:06,590 --> 00:26:08,150
So how are we going to evaluate

466
00:26:08,150 --> 00:26:10,310
a patient that
has hypertension?

467
00:26:10,430 --> 00:26:13,350
So like you learned
in DPR, you're going

468
00:26:13,350 --> 00:26:16,130
to go through your
history, the medical

469
00:26:16,130 --> 00:26:17,970
interview, as you
guys have learned.

470
00:26:18,190 --> 00:26:20,290
So the important
things are going to

471
00:26:20,290 --> 00:26:22,260
be to talk about
those risk factors.

472
00:26:22,760 --> 00:26:26,650
Then you, so you want to ask
about like any prescription

473
00:26:26,650 --> 00:26:30,790
medications, any non
-prescription NSAID use, are

474
00:26:30,790 --> 00:26:34,050
they consuming any alcohol?
Are they smoking? You want

475
00:26:34,050 --> 00:26:36,630
to know how long they've
noticed the hypertension for.

476
00:26:36,830 --> 00:26:40,930
You'll also want to ask
them if they've seen anyone

477
00:26:40,930 --> 00:26:44,150
for this already, if
they've tried taking any

478
00:26:44,150 --> 00:26:46,990
medications in the past.
Are they having any signs

479
00:26:46,990 --> 00:26:50,590
of target organ damage?
So any changes in vision?

480
00:26:51,790 --> 00:26:55,530
And then you'll also
want to know if they have

481
00:26:55,530 --> 00:26:58,070
presence of any other
known risk factors for

482
